---
// <main> content ported from skatelog-web 8e7af91:web/privacy.html. Only hrefs
// change (support.html#contact becomes /support#contact); the text must stay
// as approved, and `node scripts/check-dist.mjs --source <dir>` compares it.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Sk8tics Privacy"
  description="What Sk8tics collects, who can see it, and how to get rid of it."
>

  <p class="eyebrow">Privacy policy</p>
  <h1>What we keep, and what we don&rsquo;t.</h1>
  <p class="stamp">Effective 27 September 2026 &middot; Sk8tics, Ontario, Canada</p>

  <p class="lede">
    Sk8tics is a practice log for figure skaters and their coaches. This page
    describes every category of information the app holds, who can reach it,
    and how to remove it. It is written to be read, not to be survived.
  </p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <div class="note accent">
    <strong>There is no advertising, no analytics and no third-party tracking
    in this app.</strong> Nothing is sold. Nothing is shared for marketing. The
    only people who can see a skater&rsquo;s log are that skater and a coach
    they have personally linked with.
  </div>

  <h2>What the app collects</h2>

  <div class="scroll-x">
  <table class="data">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Category</th><th>What, exactly</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Account</td>
        <td>Email address, a username you choose, your name, whether you are
            under 18, whether you are a skater or a coach, and which discipline
            you skate.</td>
        <td>To sign you in and to show the right scale of values.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Your training</td>
        <td>Practice sessions, the marks against each element, your programmes
            and their planned content, and any notes.</td>
        <td>It is the log. This is the app.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Coach scoring</td>
        <td>Run-through scores a linked coach records for you, element by
            element, with their notes.</td>
        <td>So you can read what your coach saw.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Competition records</td>
        <td>Results imported from officially published protocols: competitor
            name, club, placement, element and component scores, and the
            individual judges&rsquo; marks, plus any notes. You can record
            your own, and a linked coach can record one for you; each record
            belongs to whoever recorded it.</td>
        <td>To keep a competition record without retyping a sheet.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Notifications</td>
        <td>A device token, if you allow notifications, and your two on/off
            preferences.</td>
        <td>To tell you when a coach has scored you.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Your first and last name, your email address and the message you send through the <a href="/support#contact">contact form</a> are emailed to us and kept in our Gmail mailbox; none of them is stored in the app or its database, except a keyed one-way hash of your email address, as the next sentence explains. To limit abuse, a keyed one-way hash of the sender&rsquo;s email and network address is kept, and deleted once it is more than 24 hours old, the next time anyone uses the form.
  </p>

  <h3>A note on competition results</h3>
  <p>
    Competition protocols are documents Skate Ontario and skatingresults.ca
    publish openly on the web. Importing one copies information that is already
    public into your own record, whether you import it yourself or a linked
    coach does it for you. Because a protocol lists everyone in a segment, you
    or your coach can also save another skater&rsquo;s result to compare
    against &mdash; exactly as you could by keeping the printed sheet.
  </p>

  <h2>What the app does not collect</h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li>No advertising identifiers, and no advertising.</li>
    <li>No analytics or usage tracking. There is no analytics SDK in the app.</li>
    <li>No location, contacts, photos, camera, microphone or health data.</li>
    <li>No payment information. The app is free and has nothing to buy.</li>
    <li>No purchase of data about you from anybody else.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who can see your information</h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li><strong>You</strong> &mdash; all of it.</li>
    <li><strong>A coach you have linked with</strong> &mdash; your log,
        programmes, scores and competition records, including the ones you
        recorded yourself, which they can read but not change. A link needs
        both sides to agree, and either side can end it. When it ends, the
        coach loses access immediately, except to the scores and competition
        records they recorded themselves.</li>
    <li><strong>Nobody else.</strong> Not other skaters, not other coaches, not
        your club.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These rules are enforced by the database, through row-level security, and
    not only by the app. A request for someone else&rsquo;s data returns nothing
    even if it is made outside the app entirely. That property is checked by an
    automated test suite on every change.
  </p>

  <h2>Who processes it for us</h2>
  <div class="scroll-x">
  <table class="data">
    <thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Supabase</td><td>Hosts the database, signs you in, and runs the server functions.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Google (Gmail)</td><td>Carries and stores the messages you send through the contact form.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Expo</td><td>Delivers app updates, and passes notifications to Apple and Google.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Apple &middot; Google</td><td>Distribute the app and deliver notifications to your device.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Skate Ontario &middot; skatingresults.ca</td><td>We <em>read</em> their published results. Nothing about you is ever sent to them.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    These are processors, not partners. None of them is given your information
    for their own purposes.
  </p>

  <h2>Skaters under 18</h2>
  <p>
    Figure skating is largely a junior sport and we expect many users to be
    minors. A younger skater&rsquo;s account collects nothing beyond what an
    adult&rsquo;s does, and is subject to exactly the same access rules.
  </p>
  <div class="note warn">
    <strong>Parents and guardians.</strong> You may ask to see, correct or
    delete a child&rsquo;s account at any time through the <a href="/support#contact">contact form</a>, and we will act
    on it without requiring the child&rsquo;s agreement. If you would rather the
    account simply not exist, say so and it will be deleted outright.
  </div>

  <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
  <p>
    Until you delete it. There is no automatic expiry, because a training log is
    only useful across seasons. Deleting your account removes everything
    described above.
  </p>

  <h2>Deleting your account</h2>
  <p>
    In the app: tap <strong>Account</strong> at the top of any screen, scroll
    to the bottom, type your username where it says <strong>Type <em>your username</em>
    to confirm</strong>, then tap <strong>Delete my account</strong>. The
    deletion is immediate and total &mdash; the sign-in goes too, not just the
    data behind it.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you can't sign in, use the <a href="/support#contact">contact form</a> with the email address on
    the account. We will reply to that address to confirm before we delete
    anything, and you do not need to install anything.
  </p>

  <h2>Your other rights</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask for a copy of your information, ask us to correct it, or ask
    what we hold. Send us a message through the <a href="/support#contact">contact form</a> and we will answer. Under Canadian privacy law
    (PIPEDA) you may also complain to the Office of the Privacy Commissioner of
    Canada if you are unhappy with how we have handled a request.
  </p>

  <h2>Security</h2>
  <p>
    Traffic is encrypted in transit. Access rules live in the database rather
    than the application, which is the meaningful difference: a bug in the app
    cannot hand out data the database will not release. Passwords are stored and
    verified by Supabase&rsquo;s authentication service and are never visible to
    us.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes in a way that affects what we collect or who can see
    it, the app will tell you rather than quietly updating this page. The
    effective date at the top always reflects the current version.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/support#contact">contact form</a> &mdash;
    for privacy questions, data requests, and anything a guardian needs.
  </p>

</Base>
